# 
# http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#DataSource
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#DataSource> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#DataSource> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A source of data used to populate the ontology with instances (e.g., Kaggle dataset, Spotify API, survey). Supports the data provenance tracking recommended by Norouzi et al. (2025) for LLM-based knowledge graph population."@en .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#DataSource> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Data Source"@en .
# 
# http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#DynamicEmotion
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#DynamicEmotion> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#DynamicEmotion> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Emotion> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#DynamicEmotion> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "An emotion state that changes over time and is tracked across multiple sessions. Inspired by the temporal behavioral modeling in Fernandez et al. (2023) GRU-RNN framework, which predicts evolving user behavior styles across sessions."@en .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#DynamicEmotion> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Dynamic Emotion"@en .
# 
# http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Effect
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Effect> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Effect> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The expected psychological or behavioral effect of a content item."@en .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Effect> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Effect"@en .
# 
# http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Emotion
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Emotion> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Emotion> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "An emotional state experienced by a user. Inspired by the UniEmotion ontology (Kim 2013) and Kim & Lee (2015) emotion ontology for recommendation, emotions are categorized as Positive, Negative, or Neutral/Factual."@en .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Emotion> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Emotion"@en .
# 
# http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#EmotionSession
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#EmotionSession> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#EmotionSession> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A bounded time window during which a user's emotional state and interactions are recorded. Enables longitudinal modeling of emotion change, supporting the dynamic emotion tracking capability."@en .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#EmotionSession> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Emotion Session"@en .
# 
# http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Environment
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Environment> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Environment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Context> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Environment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The physical or social environment of a user."@en .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Environment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Environment"@en .
# 
# http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#ExtrovertedPersonality
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#ExtrovertedPersonality> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#ExtrovertedPersonality> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Personality> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#ExtrovertedPersonality> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A personality type characterized by sociability, high energy, and preference for stimulating environments. Extroverted users tend to prefer social, upbeat, or group-oriented content (e.g., party playlists, group films)."@en .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#ExtrovertedPersonality> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Extroverted Personality"@en .
# 
# http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Feedback
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Feedback> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Feedback> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A user response to a recommendation."@en .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Feedback> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Feedback"@en .
# 
# http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Intention
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Intention> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Intention> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The intended purpose of a recommendation."@en .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Intention> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Intention"@en .
# 
# http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#IntrovertedPersonality
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#IntrovertedPersonality> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#IntrovertedPersonality> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Personality> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#IntrovertedPersonality> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A personality type characterized by preference for solitary activities, deep focus, and low-stimulation environments. Introverted users tend to prefer calm, solo content (e.g., lo-fi music, meditation, solo games)."@en .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#IntrovertedPersonality> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Introverted Personality"@en .
# 
# http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Need
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Need> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Need> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A user need that may arise from an emotional state."@en .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Need> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Need"@en .
# 
# http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#NegativeEmotion
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#NegativeEmotion> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#NegativeEmotion> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Emotion> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#NegativeEmotion> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "An emotion associated with unpleasant or unfavorable affective states. Corresponds to 'negative emotional tags' in the UniEmotion ontology (Kim 2013). Examples: stress, sadness, anxiety, tiredness."@en .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#NegativeEmotion> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Negative Emotion"@en .
# 
# http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#NeuroticlPersonality
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#NeuroticlPersonality> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#NeuroticlPersonality> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Personality> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#NeuroticlPersonality> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A personality type characterized by emotional instability and high sensitivity to stress. Neurotic users benefit most from calming, grounding content (e.g., meditation, nature soundscapes, breathing exercises)."@en .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#NeuroticlPersonality> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Neurotic Personality"@en .
# 
# http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#NeutralEmotion
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#NeutralEmotion> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#NeutralEmotion> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Emotion> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#NeutralEmotion> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A factual or neutral affective state with neither strong positive nor negative valence. Corresponds to 'factual tags' in the UniEmotion ontology (Kim 2013). Examples: calmness, focus, boredom."@en .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#NeutralEmotion> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Neutral Emotion"@en .
# 
# http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#OpennessPersonality
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#OpennessPersonality> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#OpennessPersonality> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Personality> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#OpennessPersonality> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A personality type characterized by curiosity, creativity, and appreciation of art. Users high in openness tend to enjoy diverse, novel, or artistic content (e.g., experimental music, documentaries, creative games)."@en .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#OpennessPersonality> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Openness Personality"@en .
# 
# http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Personality
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Personality> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Personality> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A personality trait based on the Big Five model that may affect recommendation suitability. Sub-classes represent the primary personality dimensions relevant to emotion-based recommendation."@en .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Personality> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Personality"@en .
# 
# http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Platform
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Platform> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Platform> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A digital platform where content may be delivered."@en .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Platform> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Platform"@en .
# 
# http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#PositiveEmotion
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#PositiveEmotion> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#PositiveEmotion> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Emotion> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#PositiveEmotion> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "An emotion associated with pleasant, favorable affective states. Corresponds to 'positive emotional tags' in the UniEmotion ontology (Kim 2013). Examples: happiness, excitement, contentment."@en .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#PositiveEmotion> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Positive Emotion"@en .
# 
# http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Preference
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Preference> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Preference> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A user's preferred content type or activity."@en .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Preference> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Preference"@en .
# 
# http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Recommendation
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Recommendation> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Recommendation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A personalized suggestion generated for a user."@en .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Recommendation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Recommendation"@en .
# 
# http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#TimeContext
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#TimeContext> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#TimeContext> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#Context> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#TimeContext> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The time-related context of a user."@en .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#TimeContext> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Time Context"@en .
# 
# http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#User
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#User> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#User> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A person who receives recommendations from the system."@en .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#User> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "User"@en .
# 
# http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#UserInteraction
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#UserInteraction> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#UserInteraction> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A single recorded interaction event between a user and a recommended content item (e.g., play, skip, complete). Used to populate ontology instances from log data, as described in the LLM-based ontology population approach of Norouzi et al. (2025)."@en .
<http://example.org/emotion-aware-recommendation#UserInteraction> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "User Interaction"@en .
